WEBVTT

NOTE SA1 Topic 9.3: Troubleshooting Method. Timings are estimates at 150 words a minute; re-sync after recording.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.600
Welcome to Topic 9.

2
00:00:01.850 --> 00:00:03.050
3, Troubleshooting Method.

3
00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:07.700
All course long you have built and
configured many Windows services.

4
00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:12.750
Now you learn how to fix them when they
break, without guessing.

5
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:17.400
You will use the six-step method from the
CompTIA Server+ objectives.

6
00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:23.650
You will find the scope of a problem and
ask what changed, keep a troubleshooting

7
00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:28.300
log, and work up the layers from the
network to performance.

8
00:00:28.550 --> 00:00:34.150
You will match common symptoms to causes,
and you will learn when to escalate.

9
00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:38.400
Here are the six steps, in the words
CompTIA uses.

10
00:00:38.650 --> 00:00:41.850
One: identify the problem and determine
the scope.

11
00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:46.500
Two: establish a theory of probable cause,
and question the obvious.

12
00:00:46.750 --> 00:00:49.950
Three: test the theory to determine the
cause.

13
00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:55.400
Four: establish a plan of action to
resolve the problem, and notify impacted

14
00:00:55.650 --> 00:00:56.850
users.

15
00:00:57.100 --> 00:00:59.500
Five: implement the solution or escalate.

16
00:00:59.750 --> 00:01:03.750
Six: verify full system functionality and,
if applicable, implement preventive

17
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:05.200
measures.

18
00:01:05.450 --> 00:01:08.650
After step six, perform a root cause
analysis.

19
00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:12.500
And during every step, document findings,
actions and outcomes.

20
00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:13.950
The order matters.

21
00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:16.200
Step one is about facts.

22
00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:21.650
Ask the user exactly what happens, and
write down the exact error text.

23
00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:23.500
Ask when it started.

24
00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:27.750
Then ask the most useful question in IT:
what changed?

25
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:31.200
Updates, new settings, a moved account,
new hardware.

26
00:01:31.450 --> 00:01:33.050
Next, determine the scope.

27
00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:34.900
One user or many?

28
00:01:35.150 --> 00:01:37.550
One computer or all of them?

29
00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:40.200
One share or the whole network?

30
00:01:40.450 --> 00:01:43.250
The answer tells you where to look.

31
00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:48.300
Step one also means collecting logs,
replicating the problem if you can,

32
00:01:48.550 --> 00:01:52.550
backing up before you change anything, and
escalating if needed.

33
00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:54.000
Now one ticket.

34
00:01:54.250 --> 00:01:55.850
CLIENT01 cannot reach srv01.

35
00:01:56.100 --> 00:01:57.300
sa1.

36
00:01:57.550 --> 00:02:01.550
lab, and step one showed that only
CLIENT01 is affected.

37
00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:07.800
Step two, the theory: one client only, so
the cause is probably on that client,

38
00:02:08.050 --> 00:02:09.250
maybe name resolution.

39
00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:15.900
Step three, the test: ping by IP address
works, but by name it goes to 192.

40
00:02:16.150 --> 00:02:17.350
168.

41
00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:18.800
50.

42
00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:20.250
99.

43
00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:26.100
Step four, the plan: remove a wrong hosts
file line and tell the user.

44
00:02:26.350 --> 00:02:29.950
Step five: delete the line and flush the
cache.

45
00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:34.200
Step six: verify by name, the way the user
works.

46
00:02:34.450 --> 00:02:36.850
A troubleshooting log keeps you honest.

47
00:02:37.100 --> 00:02:38.700
It has seven fields.

48
00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:41.750
Symptom: what happens, with the exact
message.

49
00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:45.200
Scope: who is affected and who is not.

50
00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:47.050
Theory: your probable cause.

51
00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:50.500
Test: the command you ran and its result.

52
00:02:50.750 --> 00:02:53.150
Action: what you changed, and when.

53
00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:55.800
Verify: how you proved it works.

54
00:02:56.050 --> 00:02:58.450
Prevent: what stops it happening again.

55
00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:03.500
In the example, cdiaz cannot sign in,
Get-ADUser shows LockedOut True, you

56
00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:09.350
unlock her, she signs in, and she updates
a saved password on her phone.

57
00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:13.600
Write the log as you go, not at the end.

58
00:03:13.850 --> 00:03:18.650
Problems sit in layers, and each layer
needs the layers below it.

59
00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:24.100
At the bottom is the physical link: is the
adapter up and connected?

60
00:03:24.350 --> 00:03:26.350
Then IP: ipconfig and ping.

61
00:03:26.600 --> 00:03:30.200
Then name resolution: Resolve-DnsName,
nslookup and the hosts file.

62
00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:33.250
Then authentication: can the account sign
in?

63
00:03:33.500 --> 00:03:36.300
Then authorization: share, NTFS and Group
Policy.

64
00:03:36.550 --> 00:03:40.950
Then the service itself, with Get-Service,
Event Viewer and port tests.

65
00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:43.200
And at the top, performance.

66
00:03:43.450 --> 00:03:47.450
Start at the bottom, or start where the
scope points.

67
00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:51.300
A layer that works proves every layer
below it.

68
00:03:51.550 --> 00:03:54.350
Here are the network tools on CLIENT01.

69
00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:59.400
ipconfig slash all shows the address, the
mask and the DNS server.

70
00:03:59.650 --> 00:04:01.250
If you see 169.

71
00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:06.300
254 here, no DHCP server answered, and you
can stop looking higher.

72
00:04:06.550 --> 00:04:09.350
Next, ping SRV01 by its IP address.

73
00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:15.200
If ping by address works but the name
fails, move up to name resolution.

74
00:04:15.450 --> 00:04:19.450
Then Test-NetConnection with the Port
parameter tests the service itself.

75
00:04:19.700 --> 00:04:22.100
Port 445 is SMB, file sharing.

76
00:04:22.350 --> 00:04:24.750
TcpTestSucceeded True means the port
answers.

77
00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:29.800
False means the service is stopped or a
firewall rule blocks it.

78
00:04:30.050 --> 00:04:32.050
Name resolution has a trap.

79
00:04:32.300 --> 00:04:37.500
Resolve-DnsName follows the same path as
your applications: the hosts file and the

80
00:04:37.750 --> 00:04:39.350
cache first, then DNS.

81
00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:41.200
Here it says 192.

82
00:04:41.450 --> 00:04:42.650
168.

83
00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:44.100
50.

84
00:04:44.350 --> 00:04:45.550
99.

85
00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:48.200
Add NoHostsFile, and it says 192.

86
00:04:48.450 --> 00:04:49.650
168.

87
00:04:49.900 --> 00:04:51.100
50.

88
00:04:51.350 --> 00:04:52.550
20.

89
00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:57.600
nslookup also says 20, because it always
asks the DNS server directly.

90
00:04:57.850 --> 00:05:02.250
So DNS is right, and something on the
client overrides it.

91
00:05:02.500 --> 00:05:06.500
Select-String finds the culprit: a line in
the hosts file.

92
00:05:06.750 --> 00:05:11.950
After you remove the line, run ipconfig
slash flushdns to clear the cache.

93
00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:13.800
Never trust nslookup alone.

94
00:05:14.050 --> 00:05:18.850
When someone cannot sign in, ask the
domain controller about the account.

95
00:05:19.100 --> 00:05:22.700
Get-ADUser with the LockedOut and
PasswordExpired properties shows four

96
00:05:22.950 --> 00:05:24.150
things at once.

97
00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:27.200
Enabled False means the account is
disabled.

98
00:05:27.450 --> 00:05:30.250
LockedOut True means too many bad
passwords.

99
00:05:30.500 --> 00:05:35.700
PasswordExpired True means the password
expired, or the account is set to change

100
00:05:35.950 --> 00:05:37.550
it at next logon.

101
00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:40.600
Search-ADAccount with LockedOut lists
every locked account.

102
00:05:40.850 --> 00:05:46.450
And remember the clock: if a computer is
more than five minutes off, Kerberos

103
00:05:46.700 --> 00:05:49.500
sign-in fails even with the right
password.

104
00:05:49.750 --> 00:05:55.350
The same fixes live on the Account tab in
Active Directory Users and Computers.

105
00:05:55.600 --> 00:05:59.200
Open Lakeshore, Students, right-click the
user, and select Properties.

106
00:05:59.450 --> 00:06:05.050
The Unlock account check box is available,
with the currently locked out text, only

107
00:06:05.300 --> 00:06:07.300
while the account is locked.

108
00:06:07.550 --> 00:06:09.550
Tick it and select OK.

109
00:06:09.800 --> 00:06:15.000
Under Account options, User must change
password at next logon forces a new

110
00:06:15.250 --> 00:06:16.450
password.

111
00:06:16.700 --> 00:06:19.900
Clear it if the user cannot set one.

112
00:06:20.150 --> 00:06:23.750
Account is disabled is further down the
same list.

113
00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:27.200
In PowerShell, Unlock-ADAccount,
Set-ADUser and Enable-ADAccount do the

114
00:06:27.450 --> 00:06:28.650
same jobs.

115
00:06:28.900 --> 00:06:35.300
Access denied on a share means one of two
gates said no: the share permission or

116
00:06:35.550 --> 00:06:36.750
NTFS.

117
00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:38.600
The stricter one wins.

118
00:06:38.850 --> 00:06:44.850
The Effective Access tab tells you which
gate in the Access limited by column, and

119
00:06:45.100 --> 00:06:46.700
watch for Deny entries.

120
00:06:46.950 --> 00:06:48.950
Policy not applied is different.

121
00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:50.800
Run gpresult slash r.

122
00:06:51.050 --> 00:06:57.450
If a GPO is missing from the applied list,
check the link, the OU and filtering.

123
00:06:57.700 --> 00:07:01.700
If it is filtered out, check security or
WMI filtering.

124
00:07:01.950 --> 00:07:05.550
Then run gpupdate slash force to apply it
now.

125
00:07:05.800 --> 00:07:09.400
When a server is slow, find what is busy.

126
00:07:09.650 --> 00:07:14.450
Open Resource Monitor from Server Manager,
Tools, and select the CPU tab.

127
00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:21.100
Select the CPU column heading to sort the
processes, so the busiest one is on top.

128
00:07:21.350 --> 00:07:28.150
Tick its check box: the graphs add a line
for that process, and the lower tables are

129
00:07:28.400 --> 00:07:29.600
filtered to it.

130
00:07:29.850 --> 00:07:32.650
Never end a process you cannot name.

131
00:07:32.900 --> 00:07:36.100
Right-click it for End Process, or use
Stop-Process.

132
00:07:36.350 --> 00:07:41.950
For services, Get-Service shows Running or
Stopped, and event 7036 in the System log

133
00:07:42.200 --> 00:07:44.200
records each stop and start.

134
00:07:44.450 --> 00:07:47.250
These are general patterns from the
course.

135
00:07:47.500 --> 00:07:48.700
A 169.

136
00:07:48.950 --> 00:07:52.550
254 address means the client got no DHCP
answer.

137
00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:58.800
A name that does not resolve points to the
client's DNS setting, a missing record,

138
00:07:59.050 --> 00:08:01.450
the hosts file or the cache.

139
00:08:01.700 --> 00:08:06.500
Access denied means share and NTFS
together, a Deny entry or group

140
00:08:06.750 --> 00:08:07.950
membership.

141
00:08:08.200 --> 00:08:11.800
Policy not applied: check the link, OU and
filtering.

142
00:08:12.050 --> 00:08:15.250
If the right password fails, check the
clock.

143
00:08:15.500 --> 00:08:17.500
A full disk stops services.

144
00:08:17.750 --> 00:08:22.550
Remote management needs the WinRM service,
the firewall and the right network

145
00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:24.000
profile.

146
00:08:24.250 --> 00:08:25.850
Treat them as theories.

147
00:08:26.100 --> 00:08:28.100
Sometimes step five means escalating.

148
00:08:28.350 --> 00:08:35.150
Escalate when the fix needs rights or a
system you do not own, when a vendor or

149
00:08:35.400 --> 00:08:42.200
another team must act, when many users are
down or a time limit passes, or when it

150
00:08:42.450 --> 00:08:44.450
looks like a security incident.

151
00:08:44.700 --> 00:08:46.700
Change management keeps fixes safe.

152
00:08:46.950 --> 00:08:53.750
Write a plan with a way to undo it, get
approval, back up first, notify users, and

153
00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:55.200
record what changed.

154
00:08:55.450 --> 00:09:01.450
When you escalate, hand over your log, so
the next person starts where you stopped.

155
00:09:01.700 --> 00:09:04.500
This topic leads into the course capstone.

156
00:09:04.750 --> 00:09:09.950
It is a graded skills lab at the end of
Learning Plan 9.

157
00:09:10.200 --> 00:09:15.800
You start a pod where your instructor has
planted problems from areas across the

158
00:09:16.050 --> 00:09:17.250
whole course.

159
00:09:17.500 --> 00:09:24.300
You do not know them in advance, and they
are different from the ones in Lab 9.

160
00:09:24.550 --> 00:09:25.750
3.

161
00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:32.800
For each symptom you use the six steps,
fix one thing at a time, verify, and write

162
00:09:33.050 --> 00:09:34.250
a log row.

163
00:09:34.500 --> 00:09:39.700
The capstone guide gives the time, the
rules and how it is graded.

164
00:09:39.950 --> 00:09:41.550
Four mistakes to avoid.

165
00:09:41.800 --> 00:09:44.200
One: changing several settings at once.

166
00:09:44.450 --> 00:09:49.650
Make one change, test it, and reverse it
if it did not help.

167
00:09:49.900 --> 00:09:51.900
Two: restarting the server first.

168
00:09:52.150 --> 00:09:58.150
A restart can hide the cause and destroy
evidence, so collect scope, logs and tests

169
00:09:58.400 --> 00:09:59.600
first.

170
00:09:59.850 --> 00:10:01.450
Three: trusting nslookup alone.

171
00:10:01.700 --> 00:10:06.500
It skips the hosts file and the cache, so
compare with Resolve-DnsName.

172
00:10:06.750 --> 00:10:10.350
Four: stopping as soon as it seems to
work.

173
00:10:10.600 --> 00:10:15.000
Verify the way the user works, and then
write the log.

174
00:10:15.250 --> 00:10:16.850
Time to check yourself.

175
00:10:17.100 --> 00:10:19.900
Use the six steps and the layers.

176
00:10:20.150 --> 00:10:22.550
First: only CLIENT01 has a 169.

177
00:10:22.800 --> 00:10:26.000
254 address, and other DHCP clients are
fine.

178
00:10:26.250 --> 00:10:28.650
What does the scope tell you?

179
00:10:28.900 --> 00:10:30.100
Second: ping 192.

180
00:10:30.350 --> 00:10:31.550
168.

181
00:10:31.800 --> 00:10:33.000
50.

182
00:10:33.250 --> 00:10:35.250
20 works, but ping srv01.

183
00:10:35.500 --> 00:10:36.700
sa1.

184
00:10:36.950 --> 00:10:38.150
lab fails.

185
00:10:38.400 --> 00:10:40.800
Which layer do you test next?

186
00:10:41.050 --> 00:10:43.050
Third: Get-ADUser shows LockedOut True.

187
00:10:43.300 --> 00:10:47.700
Which step are you in, and what is the
next one?

188
00:10:47.950 --> 00:10:51.950
Fourth: you changed two settings and the
problem went away.

189
00:10:52.200 --> 00:10:55.400
Why is that a problem for your log?

190
00:10:55.650 --> 00:10:57.650
Pause the video and answer.

191
00:10:57.900 --> 00:10:59.500
Here are the answers.

192
00:10:59.750 --> 00:11:05.350
One: the DHCP server works for everyone
else, so look at CLIENT01 itself: its

193
00:11:05.600 --> 00:11:09.200
adapter, its network and whether DHCP is
turned on.

194
00:11:09.450 --> 00:11:14.250
Two: the IP layer works, so test name
resolution with Resolve-DnsName, nslookup,

195
00:11:14.500 --> 00:11:17.700
the hosts file and the DNS server setting.

196
00:11:17.950 --> 00:11:22.350
Three: the test confirmed your theory, so
you finished step three.

197
00:11:22.600 --> 00:11:28.600
Step four is the plan: unlock, find the
source of the bad passwords, and notify.

198
00:11:28.850 --> 00:11:35.250
Four: you do not know which change fixed
it, so your root cause is a guess.

199
00:11:35.500 --> 00:11:38.300
Let's run the method in the pod.

200
00:11:38.550 --> 00:11:41.350
On CLIENT01, a port test to srv01.

201
00:11:41.600 --> 00:11:42.800
sa1.

202
00:11:43.050 --> 00:11:46.250
lab fails, and the remote address is 192.

203
00:11:46.500 --> 00:11:47.700
168.

204
00:11:47.950 --> 00:11:49.150
50.

205
00:11:49.400 --> 00:11:50.600
99.

206
00:11:50.850 --> 00:11:55.650
From DC01 the same test succeeds, so the
scope is just CLIENT01.

207
00:11:55.900 --> 00:11:59.100
My theory is name resolution on the
client.

208
00:11:59.350 --> 00:12:00.950
Ping by IP works.

209
00:12:01.200 --> 00:12:06.000
Resolve-DnsName says 99, but with
NoHostsFile it says 20, and so does

210
00:12:06.250 --> 00:12:07.450
nslookup.

211
00:12:07.700 --> 00:12:09.700
Select-String finds the hosts line.

212
00:12:09.950 --> 00:12:15.550
I back up the file, delete the line, flush
the cache, and test again.

213
00:12:15.800 --> 00:12:17.000
It works.

214
00:12:17.250 --> 00:12:19.650
Last, I fill in the log.

215
00:12:19.900 --> 00:12:21.100
To wrap up.

216
00:12:21.350 --> 00:12:26.550
Use the six steps in order, and finish
with a root cause analysis.

217
00:12:26.800 --> 00:12:29.200
Scope and what changed come first.

218
00:12:29.450 --> 00:12:34.650
Log the symptom, scope, theory, test,
action, verify and prevent fields as you

219
00:12:34.900 --> 00:12:36.100
go.

220
00:12:36.350 --> 00:12:40.750
Work up the layers with the right tool for
each one.

221
00:12:41.000 --> 00:12:45.400
Make one change at a time, and escalate
with your log.

222
00:12:45.650 --> 00:12:46.850
In Lab 9.

223
00:12:47.100 --> 00:12:52.700
3, about 120 minutes and practice only,
you break five things on purpose, diagnose

224
00:12:52.950 --> 00:12:57.750
each one with the six steps, and fill in
the troubleshooting log.
